tebelskis@msn.com (Joe Tebelskis) wrote in message news:<7340a1d5.0411171412.21db0f40@posting.google.com>...
> In a recent game I encountered and defeated my own ghost from an
> earlier game.  Is it considered unethical to refer to my notes from
> the end of that game to fully identify the items left at my own grave?

Thanks, everyone, for your opinions.  The consensus seems to be that
yes, it's unethical to refer to one's detailed notes from a previous
game, because that information is theoretically unknowable to a
character, and can easily be ignored (unlike any short-term memories
you may have of the game).  It's unethical to exploit your notes to
recover from amnesia for exactly the same reason, i.e., the character
shouldn't unrealistically recall what has explicitly been forgotten. 
By contrast, it *is* arguably ethical to make statistical inferences
based on general knowledge of the game, e.g., a Hawaiian T-shirt,
expensive camera, and poor armor suggest that the ghost was probably a
tourist, and that his four stacked scrolls are therefore probably
magic mapping.

I agree with Kevin Wayne, though, that casual players may be held to a
lower standard, for the sake of their learning more about the game.  I
consider myself an intermediate player, never having gotten deeper
than 12 levels in a real game, although I've ascended twice in Explore
mode (after 25+ deaths) to get the lay of the land.  After having
initially developed my own incomplete "spoilers" from raw playing, I
now use the public spoilers extensively, as I need all the help I can
get :-) if I'm ever going to beat this amazing game before I die (of
cancer within the next couple of years).

So, as an intermediate casual player, I consider it borderline
unethical that I referred to my notes from an earlier game to identify
my own ghost's inventory.  I did it, but I'm not proud of it.  From
that experience, I learned that items' appearances are translated
between games (e.g. a Wand of Death that used to be silver is now
ebony, so /oDigging never becomes /oWishing); and that bones in the
Mines are generated on a level relative to the Mine Entrance, rather
than on an absolute level; and my "cheating" enabled me to identify
valuable gems more readily, which benefitted my luck with unicorns. 
(But the game ended soon anyway, after a Mordor orc zapped
/oCreateMonster to sic a zoo on me....)

BTW, my notes on each game (saved individually) include things like
which levels contain various altars, unicorns, shops, etc.; periodic
records of my stats and intrinsics (to help identify +1 rings, track
my vulnerabilities, timeouts, etc.); and notable events (e.g. how I
survived a given situation).  My notes don't relate appearances to
identities; I leave that up to the game (so amnesia is enforced).  The
"detailed notes" that helped me identify my ghost's inventory was
actually the Playback file for that game, which would not be normally
be available to me.  Mea culpa.

